[George] Declaring, Taft is the most capable man for president in the country.

38
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] Driven forward by Roosevelt and his wife Nellie, Taft defeated William Jennings Bryan in a comfortable victory.

39
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:27.680
[George] Yet on the morning of his inauguration in March 1909, during a ferocious blizzard that knocked out telegraph lines across the Capitol, Taft looked out the window of the White House and remarked with poignant melancholy, I am glad to be going into the White House.

40
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.080
[George] But I would much prefer to be going into the Supreme Court.

41
00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:42.200
[George] Backslash and backslash and Roosevelt's relentless pressure overcame Taft's reluctance, convincing the judge that he had a sacred duty to preserve the progressive policies of the administration.

42
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:50.360
[George] Helen Taft and Roosevelt joined forces, leaving the reluctant jurist with no honorable escape from the presidential nomination.

43
00:05:50.360 --> 00:06:00.440
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft accepted the nomination with a heavy heart, knowing that his temperament was suited for the bench rather than the partisan combat of the White House.

44
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:08.600
[George] History often remembers Theodore Roosevelt as the great trust buster, but the historical record tells a startlingly different story.

45
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:14.360
[George] In seven and a half years in office, Roosevelt filed 44 antitrust lawsuits.

46
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:22.520
[George] In just four years, President William Howard Taft filed 90 antitrust suits, more than double Roosevelt's total.

47
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:23.400
[George] While T.R.

48
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:29.160
[George] believed in distinguishing between good trusts and bad trusts through personal presidential discretion.

49
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:38.280
[George] Judge Taft operated strictly under the letter of the law, insisting that all corporate monopolies that restrained trade must be prosecuted impartially.

50
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:46.680
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft's Justice Department secured the historic Supreme Court decisions in 1911 that shattered John D.

51
00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:55.080
[George] Rockefeller's standard oil monopoly into 34 independent companies, and dissolved the massive American Tobacco Company Trust.

52
00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:05.560
[George] Taft also filed a major antitrust suit against United States Steel, demonstrating that even the mightiest financial syndicates in the country were not immune to federal law.

53
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:12.360
[George] Taft's aggressive antitrust enforcement proved that his devotion to the law was far more than conservative rhetoric.

54
00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft's strict legalism meant that he gave no quarter to corporate trusts.

55
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.200
[George] Insisting that every combination and restraint of trade must be prosecuted regardless of political influence.

56
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:34.880
[George] He rejected Roosevelt's belief that a president should decide between good and bad trusts, arguing that the law must apply equally to all corporate monopolies.

57
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:46.560
[George] The Justice Department prosecuted rail cartels, meatpacking trusts, and industrial syndicates with relentless efficiency.

58
00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:55.520
[George] Despite facing relentless attacks from progressive insurgents who viewed him as a reactionary, Taft's domestic record was remarkably progressive.

59
00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:09.360
[George] Under Taft, the Man Elkins Act of 1910 significantly expanded the powers of the Interstate Commerce Commission, granting it authority to regulate telephone, telegraph, and wireless communications for the first time.

60
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:19.680
[George] Taft created the United States Postal Savings System, providing ordinary working immigrants with secure, government-backed savings accounts at local post offices.

61
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:32.560
[George] Furthermore, Taft championed the creation of the Children's Bureau to investigate infant mortality, child labor, and orphanages, and established the Federal Department of Labor.

62
00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:45.120
[George] He sponsored the 16th Amendment to the United States Constitution, establishing the federal income tax, and the 17th Amendment, providing for the direct election of United States senators by the people.

63
00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:54.840
[George] Taft was also a pioneering environmental steward, withdrawing more public lands for conservation and oil reserves in four years than Roosevelt had in seven.

64
00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.040
[George] Backslash N backslash N under Taft's leadership.

65
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:06.600
[George] The creation of the Children's Bureau and the Department of Labor gave American working families their first institutional voice in the federal government.

66
00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:15.960
[George] He also signed the Postal Savings Act, providing secure banking services to millions of newly arrived immigrants who distrusted commercial banks.

67
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:27.400
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft championed the direct election of senators and the progressive federal income tax, embedding enduring structural reforms into the United States Constitution.

68
00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:35.240
[George] In foreign policy, Taft replaced Roosevelt's big stick with a modern commercial doctrine known as dollar diplomacy.

69
00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:54.440
[George] Taft believed that the United States should use its immense private capital and commercial investment, rather than military gunboats, to promote stability, economic development, and international peace in Latin America and East Asia, backslash and backslash and Taft summarized his policy with typical clarity.

70
00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:59.560
[George] The policy of this administration is characterized as substituting dollars for bullets.

71
00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:13.720
[George] Under Taft, American banking syndicates reorganized the foreign debts of Honduras, Nicaragua, and the Dominican Republic, aiming to keep European powers from using loan defaults as a pretext for military intervention.

72
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:28.600
[George] In China, Taft pushed for American bankers to participate in the International Railroad Consortia, defending the open-door policy with financial leverage, while critics accused Taft of using foreign policy to protect Wall Street interests.

73
00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:35.080
[George] Taft's approach established the foundational model for 20th-century American foreign economic assistance.

74
00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:43.240
[George] Backslash and backslash and dollar diplomacy represented an innovative effort to replace military intervention with economic partnership.

75
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.000
[George] Laying the foundation for modern American international development policy.

76
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:55.120
[George] Taft believed that American private investment could build roads, harbors, and hospitals across Latin America.

77
00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:09.520
[George] Fostering stability and democracy, backslash and backslash and by encouraging American capital to compete with European loans in Asia, Taft aimed to safeguard the territorial independence of China while promoting peaceful commerce.

78
00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:17.680
[George] Despite his substantial accomplishments, Taft's presidency was overshadowed by the bitter factional war that tore his party apart.

79
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.880
[George] Taft was an administrator, not a politician.

80
00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:29.760
[George] He hated campaigning, refused to flatter journalists, and lacked the theatrical charisma necessary to communicate his achievements to the public.

81
00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:35.120
[George] Crushed between the radical progressives of his own party and the rising Democratic tide.

82
00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:41.520
[George] Taft was soundly defeated in the three-way election of 1912, carrying only Utah and Vermont.

83
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:47.360
[George] Backslash and backslash and yet Taft accepted his defeat with serene, cheerful relief.

84
00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.440
[George] The truth is, Taft remarked with his booming, infectious chuckle, I am glad to be out of it.

85
00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.640
[George] The White House was the loneliest place in the world.

86
00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:04.480
[George] Taft became a beloved professor of constitutional law at Yale Law School, losing £70 and regaining his vibrant health.

87
00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:09.520
[George] Then, in June 1921, Destiny finally fulfilled his true calling.

88
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.040
[George] President Warren G.

